### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants the second character that appears exactly once in a string. This tests LinkedHashMap for ordered frequency counting, entry filtering, and skip-then-findFirst.

### How do you find the second non-repeating character?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class SecondNonRepeating {
    public static Optional<Character> secondNonRepeating(String s) {
        Map<Character, Long> freq = s.chars()
            .mapToObj(c -> Character.toLowerCase((char) c))
            .filter(Character::isLetter)
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                c -> c,
                LinkedHashMap::new,
                Collectors.counting()));

        return freq.entrySet().stream()
            .filter(e -> e.getValue() == 1)
            .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
            .skip(1)
            .findFirst();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code = "NATIONAL BANK";
        Optional<Character> result = secondNonRepeating(code);
        System.out.println("Second non-repeating: "
            + result.map(String::valueOf).orElse("none"));
    }
}
```

```text
Second non-repeating: i
```

In "national bank" (lowercase), the unique characters in first-seen order are t, i, o, l, b, k. The second one is 'i'.

### Why is LinkedHashMap essential?

With a plain HashMap, iteration order is arbitrary, so 'second' would be meaningless.

```java
// WRONG: HashMap gives arbitrary order
Map<Character, Long> unordered = s.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(c -> c, Collectors.counting()));
// skip(1) on an arbitrary order gives an arbitrary character

// CORRECT: LinkedHashMap preserves insertion (first-seen) order
Map<Character, Long> ordered = s.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        c -> c, LinkedHashMap::new, Collectors.counting()));
```

### How do you generalize to the Nth non-repeating character?

```java
public static Optional<Character> nthNonRepeating(String s, int n) {
    if (n < 1) throw new IllegalArgumentException("n must be >= 1");

    Map<Character, Long> freq = s.chars()
        .mapToObj(c -> Character.toLowerCase((char) c))
        .filter(Character::isLetter)
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
            c -> c, LinkedHashMap::new, Collectors.counting()));

    return freq.entrySet().stream()
        .filter(e -> e.getValue() == 1)
        .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
        .skip(n - 1)
        .findFirst();
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

```java
// All characters repeat: no unique characters at all
Optional<Character> allRepeat = secondNonRepeating("aabbcc");
System.out.println(allRepeat.isPresent());  // false

// Only one unique character: second does not exist
Optional<Character> oneUnique = secondNonRepeating("aabbc");
System.out.println(oneUnique.isPresent());  // false

// Empty string: no characters at all
Optional<Character> empty = secondNonRepeating("");
System.out.println(empty.isPresent());  // false
```

### How is this different from finding the first non-repeating character?

| Operation | Pipeline ending | Result |
|---|---|---|
| First non-repeating | filter(count == 1).findFirst() | First unique in appearance order |
| Second non-repeating | filter(count == 1).skip(1).findFirst() | Second unique in appearance order |
| Nth non-repeating | filter(count == 1).skip(n-1).findFirst() | Nth unique in appearance order |

The only difference is the skip value.

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using HashMap instead of LinkedHashMap.

```java
// WRONG: HashMap has no ordering guarantee
Map<Character, Long> wrong = s.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(c -> c, Collectors.counting()));
// "second" is meaningless without order

// CORRECT: LinkedHashMap preserves first-seen order
Map<Character, Long> correct = s.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        c -> c, LinkedHashMap::new, Collectors.counting()));
```

Mistake: filtering for count > 1 instead of count == 1.

```java
// WRONG: count > 1 gives DUPLICATE characters, not unique ones
.filter(e -> e.getValue() > 1)

// CORRECT: count == 1 gives NON-REPEATING characters
.filter(e -> e.getValue() == 1)
```

### What about performance?

The approach is linear overall: one pass to count, one pass to filter and skip. The LinkedHashMap's iteration is O(n) where n is the number of distinct characters, which is at most the alphabet size. For very long strings, this is efficient.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you find the first repeating character?" Filter for count > 1 and take findFirst from the LinkedHashMap.
- "How do you handle Unicode characters?" Use codePoints() instead of chars().
- "What if you need both the character and its position?" Track positions in a custom data structure.
